<title>GERMANY: BONN SAYS EMU DELAY STORY &quot;PURE SPECULATION&quot;.</title>
<headline>BONN SAYS EMU DELAY STORY &quot;PURE SPECULATION&quot;.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1997-08-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Bonn on Wednesday dismissed as &quot;pure speculation&quot; a newspaper article saying Germany and France might delay European economic and monetary union (EMU) for a year if Britain agreed to join the starting lineup.</p>
<p>The unsourced commentary in the London Evening Standard knocked the dollar and sterling against the mark in New York foreign exchange trading.</p>
<p>A spokesman for Chancellor Helmut Kohl's government said Germany's position remained unchanged. Both the timetable and the criteria must be met for EMU to go ahead, he said.</p>
<p>A British treasury spokesman also poured cold water on the article: &quot;We won't dignify this story with a quote,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>EMU is scheduled to begin at the start of 1999, with leaders due to decide in the first half of next year which countries qualify under the convergence criteria stipulated in the Maastricht treaty.</p>
<p>-- Douglas Busvine, Bonn newsroom, 49-228-26097150</p>
